    icon: '🔐',
    tldr: "Singapore's data protection law: consent, purpose limitation, protection, and a 3-day clock to notify the PDPC of notifiable breaches.",
    body: [
      "The Personal Data Protection Act 2012 (PDPA) governs how private-sector organisations in Singapore collect, use and disclose personal data, and is administered by the Personal Data Protection Commission (PDPC). Core obligations include consent (for collection, use and disclosure, unless an exception applies), purpose limitation (use data only for purposes a reasonable person would consider appropriate, and that you've notified), and protection (make reasonable security arrangements). Others cover access and correction, accuracy, retention, overseas transfers and accountability, including appointing a Data Protection Officer.",
      "Breach notification is mandatory. Once an organisation assesses that a data breach is notifiable, meaning it's likely to cause significant harm to the people affected or is of significant scale (500 or more individuals), it must notify the PDPC within 3 calendar days. Affected individuals must also be notified where the breach is likely to cause them significant harm.",
      "For a TPM, PDPA shows up as design and process work: data inventories, consent flows, retention rules, access controls, vendor contracts and a breach-response runbook. Public agencies are not covered by the PDPA; they follow separate government data rules.",
    ],
    inPractice: [
      "Map the data at design time: what personal data, where it's stored, who can access it, how long you keep it, and whether it leaves Singapore.",
      "Bring the DPO into design review, not launch week; consent wording and retention rules change product and engineering scope.",
      "Add PDPA items to the launch checklist: notice and consent text approved, access reviewed, test data masked, retention and deletion jobs live, vendor data terms signed.",
      "Rehearse breach response: who assesses, who decides it's notifiable, who drafts the PDPC and customer notices. A 3-day clock leaves no time to work out the RACI.",
      "Never copy production personal data into lower environments without approved masking.",
    ],
    tlTrap: "Assuming security equals compliance. Encryption and access control address the protection obligation, but consent, purpose and retention are product and process decisions. Reusing transaction data for a new marketing model, for example, may need fresh consent or a valid exception.",
