    id: 'ai-delivery',
    title: 'AI-assisted delivery (humans still merge)',
    category: 'Execution',
    icon: '🤖',
    tldr: "AI assistants speed up reading, debugging and boilerplate. Guardrails keep changes small and tested, and a human owns every merge.",
    body: [
      "Most engineering teams now use AI coding assistants, and some run agents that open pull requests by themselves. Used well, they compress the slow, tedious parts of delivery. Used carelessly, they produce big, plausible-looking diffs faster than anyone can review them, and the bill arrives as incidents and rework.",
      "The guardrails are boring on purpose. A context file in each repo tells the assistant the house rules: architecture, conventions, test commands, no-go zones. Changes stay small enough to review properly, every change comes with tests, existing patterns beat clever new ones, and a named human is accountable for every merge. The model doesn't carry the pager.",
      "The real savings come from investigation (reading an unfamiliar service, tracing a call path), debugging, documentation, boilerplate and onboarding. They're thin on novel design and fuzzy requirements, and zero on time spent waiting for a decision, a review or another team, which is where most critical paths actually run.",
      "Measure it like any process change: against a baseline, with delivery metrics such as cycle time, review load, change failure rate and escaped defects. Lines of AI-written code measure usage, not value, and gut feel misleads: one small randomised study in 2025 found experienced developers were slower with AI tools on codebases they knew well, while believing they'd been faster.",
    ],
    inPractice: [
      "Check that every repo on the program has a context file the assistant reads (rules, patterns, test commands, no-go areas), owned and reviewed like code.",
      "Agree norms with the leads: small PRs, tests with every change, and an author who can explain every line, whoever (or whatever) typed it.",
      "Baseline before rollout: a few weeks of cycle time, review wait, change failure rate and escaped defects. Compare team by team afterwards, and report where it didn't help.",
      "Turn good answers into team knowledge. When the assistant explains a gnarly module well, have an engineer verify it and commit it to the repo's docs, rather than leaving it in one person's chat history.",
      "Estimate as usual, then discount only the coding share, and only with evidence: if coding is 30% of cycle time, doubling its speed saves 15% at best. Review, testing and approvals stay full size.",
    ],
